{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>כְּאֶזְרָח מִכֶּם</b> [like a native among you]. Here HaShem revealed His secret, that all the souls of His holy people were embedded in Egypt among the nations, and this is what He said: ״כְּאֶזְרָח מִכֶּם״ and so forth. Now if you say: how can the seed of wicked people, whose fathers were gentiles, be equated to a native whose fathers were the beloved of HaShem? — for this He said <b>כִּי גֵרִים הֱיִיתֶם בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרָיִם</b> [for you were strangers in the land of Egypt] — meaning, their souls were intermingled within the klipot of Egypt. See what I explained in parashat Yitro (Exodus 20:2) on the verse ״אָנֹכִי HaShem… אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרָיִם״ and so forth. And likewise this convert — his soul was within the klipot, and now it shone forth in the world and returned to its root. Accordingly, there is reason to find a praiseworthy implication in ״כִּי גֵרִים״ and so forth, for if it refers to the gerut of the bodies, the reason is not precise, since a convert who comes from among the nations may differ in that he may not be beloved, on account of the distinction that exists in the change of families — for not all the families of the gentiles are like the house of Israel."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:40.431152247Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}